This won&#39;t be acceptable, though: the value would be shown twice (the item and the progress bar), and even if you hide the progress bar text (by calling [`QProgressBar.setFormat()`](https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qprogressbar.html#format-prop) with an empty string), the filled region will eventually cover the item text as soon as the value is closer to 100.

In general, using widgets for such a purpose is discouraged, and proper item delegates should be used instead. Not only they require much less resources (you don&#39;t need a widget for each cell), but they make value updates much more direct and consistent.

To achieve this, the value should be set as a number (not a string), and a custom user role must be used in order to show different progress bar colors.

```py
ProgressColorRole = Qt.UserRole + 1

class ProgressDelegate(QStyledItemDelegate):
    def paint(self, qp, opt, index):
        progress = index.data()
        if isinstance(progress, (int, float)):
            color = index.data(ProgressColorRole)
            if not isinstance(color, QColor) or not color.isValid():
                color = QApplication.palette().highlight()
            rect = QRectF(opt.rect)
            rect.setWidth(rect.width() * progress / 100)
            qp.save()
            qp.setPen(Qt.NoPen)
            qp.setBrush(color)
            qp.drawRect(rect)
            qp.restore()
        super().paint(qp, opt, index)


